{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Code Review PR Checklist & Taxonomy\n\nWhen performing code reviews, evaluate changes using this structured checklist and severity taxonomy.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Finding Severity Taxonomy\n\n| Severity | Label | Definition | Examples |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **P0** | **Critical (Blocker)** | Security vulnerabilities, data loss risks, production crashes, secret leakage. Must be fixed before merging. | Hardcoded tokens, unhandled NullPointer on critical path, SQL injection, broken auth check. |\n| **P1** | **High (Must Address)** | Logic bugs, breaking API changes, missing error handling on external calls, race conditions, lack of tests for complex logic. | Swallowed exceptions, broken retry loop, missing backward compatibility in API schema. |\n| **P2** | **Medium (Recommended)** | Performance inefficiencies, code duplication, sub-optimal data structures, non-idiomatic style. | Unnecessary DB query inside loop, missing index, missing parameter validation. |\n| **P3** | **Low (Nit / Suggestion)** | Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs, minor doc polish. | Variable naming clarity, typos in docstrings, cleaning leftover TODO comments. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Comprehensive Review Checklist\n\n### A. Logic & Correctness\n- [ ] **Edge Cases**: Does the code handle null/nil/undefined, empty collections, zero values, boundary numbers, and unexpected inputs?\n- [ ] **State & Concurrency**: Are shared state mutations thread-safe? Are locks acquired/released properly without deadlock risks?\n- [ ] **Control Flow**: Are all conditional branches covered? Do loops have guaranteed termination conditions?\n- [ ] **Error Propagation**: Are errors caught and logged/surfaced appropriately, rather than swallowed silently (`catch (Exception e) {}`)?\n\n### B. Security & Credentials\n- [ ] **No Hardcoded Secrets**: Are API keys, passwords, bearer tokens, or private keys excluded from code and config?\n- [ ] **Input Validation & Sanitization**: Are external inputs validated before processing or passing to system shells, DB queries, or HTML rendering?\n- [ ] **Authentication & Authorization**: Do endpoints verify user identity and check permissions before executing state changes?\n- [ ] **Safe Dependencies**: Are newly added packages pinned to trusted versions without known vulnerabilities?\n\n### C. Architecture & API Design\n- [ ] **Preserve API Contracts**: Are public method signatures, REST endpoints, and schema definitions backward compatible?\n- [ ] **Single Responsibility**: Are modules, classes, and functions focused on a clear, single responsibility?\n- [ ] **Coupling & Cohesion**: Is the new code properly modularized without introducing circular dependencies or leaky abstractions?\n- [ ] **Idempotence**: Are retry loops or state-changing operations safe to re-execute on transient failures?\n\n### D. Performance & Resource Efficiency\n- [ ] **N+1 Queries**: Are database or remote API calls executed in batches rather than inside loops?\n- [ ] **Resource Cleanup**: Are file handles, database connections, streams, and web sockets closed explicitly (or managed via try-with-resources / context managers)?\n- [ ] **Memory & Allocation**: Are large datasets streamed or paginated rather than loaded fully into memory at once?\n\n### E. Test Coverage & Quality\n- [ ] **Test Coverage**: Do new features or bug fixes include corresponding unit or integration tests?\n- [ ] **Test Quality**: Do tests verify actual assertions rather than just checking that code runs without throwing?\n- [ ] **Edge Case Tests**: Are failure modes, invalid inputs, and error handlers covered by unit tests?\n\n---\n\n## 3. Review Feedback Guidelines\n\n1. **Be Specific**: Always reference the exact file line number, function name, and context.\n2. **Explain the \"Why\"**: Don't just say \"change this\". Explain the risk or benefit (e.g., \"This could lead to a race condition if two threads call `transact!` simultaneously...\").\n3. **Provide Concrete Fixes**: Offer concrete, copy-pasteable diff snippets where appropriate.\n4. **Acknowledge Good Work**: Highlight well-crafted solutions, thorough tests, or clean refactorings.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790343000000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-revision.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '1,340p'; nl -ba collab/1790342400000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-review.gpt-6-sol.findings.md | sed -n '1,220p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790343000000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-revision.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '1,340p'; nl -ba collab/1790342400000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-review.gpt-6-sol.findings.md | sed -n '1,220p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-25 13:56:58 GMT\n     2\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-25 20:56:58 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n     3\t\n     4\tRead-only revision; no files edited. I verified every location codex cited. Codex is right on the substance of all four P1s and the P2. On two details it overstates, and I hold those with evidence below. Rule R survives and becomes complete under one condition, stated in item 5. I withdraw the validator-only M2 fallback.\n     5\t\n     6\t## Verdicts on codex's factual claims\n     7\t\n     8\t**PARTLY, \"def is already a special form\": ACCEPT.** Yang dispatches on the head symbol before local lookup (clojure.cljc:137) and then lowers to an ordinary application (clojure.cljc:157). My phrasing was \"the front end treats it as special; the evaluators do not,\" which is codex's reading too. No revision needed beyond precision.\n     9\t\n    10\t**TRUE, ordinary primitive: agreed.** vm.cljc:194.\n    11\t\n    12\t**TRUE narrowly, shared resolver: ACCEPT the narrowing.** Direct store instructions never touch the resolver (walker 419 to 438; stack and register `:store-put` cases). My design already treated them separately, but the sentence \"the shared resolver simply never sees the name\" was too broad.\n    13\t\n    14\t**FALSE as stated, \"only primitives raise effects, all store keys are literal\": PARTLY.** Two halves. First half: a program closure returning an effect-shaped map is only a value, and the walker dispatches effects only for host functions (ast_walker.cljc:189). That holds. But a composition-supplied host function is a primitive by registry, and it may return an effect with any key, since the effect predicate is just \"map with an effect key\" (module.cljc:77) and the dispatcher writes whatever key it is handed (engine.cljc:511). Second half: the literal-key claim holds for portable syntax only. The `:vm/store-update` node is walker-only, absent from the row grammar (vm.cljc:795 to 822), and unsupported by de Bruijn resolution (debruijn_resolve.cljc:71), so it never enters an image, but it does exist on the raw walker path. Codex's correction stands for both halves. Revised wording: every store write that can appear in an admitted image has a literal key; host-registry effects and walker-only nodes are outside the image and are guarded at dispatch.\n    15\t\n    16\t## Verdicts on the findings\n    17\t\n    18\t**P1, macro incoming store: ACCEPT.** `macro-of` reads the operator name straight from the context store (macro.cljc:973 to 980) and `expand-node` consults it before anything else (macro.cljc:1105). The store arrives through `make-ctx` (macro.cljc:1224), through the previous batch's next-store (macro.cljc:1161), and through `seed-store` (macro.cljc:1268); the REPL seeds one from standard forms (repl.cljc:470 to 483). Refusing at harvest only covers new definitions. Revision: `make-ctx` and `expand-batch` refuse a context whose store binds a reserved name, with reason `:reserved-name`, before any expansion; a `:next-store` carrying one is impossible once harvest refuses, but the admission check runs anyway so a restored or hand-built store is caught. Test: seed a store with a macro named `yin/def`, then expand a batch containing a definition; the batch is refused, not rewritten.\n    19\t\n    20\t**P1, direct admission paths: ACCEPT the gap, PARTLY on the row-local point.** The walker takes datom batches through `vm-load-program` (ast_walker.cljc:709) and row sets through `vm-load-rows` (ast_walker.cljc:725), and its raw-AST fast path resolves variables directly (ast_walker.cljc:628). None passes the linker. Callers today include the REPL, dao.await, the expander's own transformer VM (macro.cljc:729), and the semantic loader (semantic.cljc:670). \"Every image reaching any engine has passed linker validation\" was false and is withdrawn. On the row-local point codex is right that a content-addressed `[:variable yin/def]` row is shared by every occurrence, so a per-row check cannot know the use site. But the whole-tree occurrence machinery already exists: the linker's AST scanners are occurrence-path queries over `vm/occurrences` (linker.cljc:289 to 319), and `vm/validate-rows` is the whole-tree step 4 validator. The occurrence-aware check goes there and in `semantic-bytecode->ast`, which `vm-load-rows` already runs (ast_walker.cljc:726 docstring). Revised enforcement table is in item 1.\n    21\t\n    22\t**P1, effect dispatch and supplied stores: ACCEPT.** A host callable can emit a store-put effect keyed `yin/def` and the dispatcher accepts it (engine.cljc:511). Supplied initial stores exist on all three constructors (stack.cljc:149, register.cljc:164, vm.cljc:1693 via the pair store). Codex is also right that this cannot shadow a syntactic definition; it only broke my \"store never holds the key\" claim. Revision: the reserved-key check moves into `engine/handle-effect` for `:vm/store-put`, into every direct store instruction, and into each `create-vm` for a supplied store. Host effects with computed keys keep the incomplete-discovery treatment.\n    23\t\n    24\t**P1, contract versioning for all four: ACCEPT.** UCF 7.3.3 says a changed transition or added opcode is a new revision (UCF 262 to 274). Rule R changes the walker's application transition, adds a semantic opcode, and adds a stack and register opcode. The linker records advertise \"v2\", \"v2\", \"b1\", \"r1\" (M2 linker.cljc:687, 705, 727, 747), the manifests carry them per format (linker.md 1525), and the derivation record stamps \"v2\" (ledger.cljc:48). All four must move. Plan in item 2. One thing I found while checking: no lowering path in the tree compares an incoming stamp against its own. The stamp is produced at lift (ucf.cljc:221) and mentioned in `completion`, but no loader refuses `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`. That is a pre-existing defect the versioning plan depends on, and I list it as such.\n    25\t\n    26\t**P2, quoted data: ACCEPT.** A literal symbol is data (clojure.cljc:140). Banning `(quote yin/def)` was unnecessary; my earlier text also said as much in the caveat, but the headline sentence conflated the roles. The rule is restated by syntactic role in item 1.\n    27\t\n    28\t**Codex's other remarks.** It agrees on rejecting primitives-first lookup, agrees the round 3 and 4 filters go once R is complete, agrees the round 2 and 3 recognition and position logic stay, and treats a dedicated define node as acceptable but not required. I keep the application shape, with codex's condition adopted: one shared recognizer serves the validators, the linearizers, and the walker.\n    29\t\n    30\t## 1. Rule R, revised\n    31\t\n    32\t`yin/def` is syntax, never a name. By syntactic role:\n    33\t\n    34\t- **Variable occurrence.** A `:variable` row naming `yin/def` is legal only as the operator of an `:application` with exactly two operands whose first operand is a `:literal` symbol other than `yin/def`. Any other variable occurrence is refused. The same holds for a `:var yin/def` instruction, a `:load-free yin/def`, and a `[:load-free rd yin/def]`: none is legal at all after the lowering change, because the definition form lowers to `:define`.\n    35\t- **Binder.** `yin/def` may not appear in any `:lambda` params, in any de Bruijn resolved frame, or in a supplied free env.\n    36\t- **Store key.** `yin/def` may not be the key of `:vm/store-put`, `:vm/store-get`, `:vm/store-update`, any `:store-put` or `:store-get` instruction, any store-put effect reaching the dispatcher, or any entry in a supplied initial store or module-store snapshot.\n    37\t- **Definition and macro key.** `(yin/def 'yin/def _)` is refused, and so is a harvested or stored macro under that name.\n    38\t- **Quoted data.** A `:literal` whose value is the symbol `yin/def` is ordinary data and is allowed anywhere a literal is, including as the value operand of a definition.\n    39\t\n    40\tEnforcement boundaries, each stating the same rule through one shared recognizer in `yin.vm`:\n    41\t\n    42\t| Boundary | Where | When |\n    43\t|---|---|---|\n    44\t| Row set admission | `vm/validate-rows` and `semantic-bytecode->ast`, occurrence-aware | load |\n    45\t| Datom batch admission | `vm/datoms->ast` followed by the same whole-tree check | load |\n    46\t| Raw map AST | walker `:application`, `:variable`, `:lambda`, store node cases | transition |\n    47\t| Semantic vector | `code/well-formed-vector?` for `:var`, `:store-*` keys, `:define` shape | load |\n    48\t| Stack and register images | `image-defect` and `register-image-defect` | load |\n    49\t| Linker | the four format records' row-defect and validate slots, which are the functions above | link, before any engine |\n    50\t| De Bruijn resolution | resolver refuses a reserved param or a reserved free name outside definition shape | lowering |\n    51\t| Expander | `make-ctx` and `expand-batch` refuse a store binding the name; harvest refuses the key; the transformer VM is a walker and inherits the transition checks | admission |\n    52\t| Effect dispatch | `engine/handle-effect` refuses a store-put keyed `yin/def` | transition |\n    53\t| Constructors | each `create-vm` refuses a supplied store, free env, or primitive registry binding the name | construction |\n    54\t\n    55\tLoad-time refusals cover everything that is a fact of the code or of supplied state. Transition-time refusals are the residue for paths with no load step: the raw map AST fast path and host effects. A transition-time refusal throws with the same `:reserved-name` data.\n    56\t\n    57\t## 2. Contract versioning\n    58\t\n    59\tNew revisions: AST \"v3\", semantic \"v3\", stack \"b2\", register \"r2\". The revision text for each names the definition form and, for the three lowered formats, the `:define` instruction: semantic `:define key` acting on the value register, stack `[:define key]` popping the value and pushing it back, register `[:define rd key rs]`. Update: the four linker format records, the manifest's per-format contracts entry (linker.md 8.1), the derivation profile record (ledger.cljc:42 to 49), the UCF stamp constant (ucf.cljc:46), and the completion default (completion.cljc:697).\n    60\t\n    61\tOld-contract images. For the three lowered formats an old image contains `:var yin/def` or `:load-free yin/def`, which the new validator refuses as a reserved variable occurrence, so old images are refused by validation alone, with no stamp needed. For the AST format an old image is byte-identical to a new one; only its interpretation changed, so refusal is by stamp only: the manifest's contracts entry and the linker's admission check (linker.cljc:1190) already refuse a mismatch, and a request that names no contract must now be refused rather than admitted, which is a one-line change to that check. For continuations, the lowering paths must actually compare the stamp and answer `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`, which no loader does today; that pre-existing defect becomes a blocker for this change. No compatible loader is offered. Clean break, per the no-backward-compatibility rule for this repo.\n    62\t\n    63\t## 3. Documents to update\n    64\t\n    65\t- UCF: 7.3.3 revision list gains reserved operators; 7.5.2 bullet and 7.11 blocker drop the `yin/def` profile; 7.6.1 discovery text for store keys.\n    66\t- linker.md: 4.1 line 309, 4.2 5b, the four records in section 5, 8.1 contracts, the store-isolation clause is unchanged, section 11 criteria.\n    67\t- yin.vm.semantic.md 2.4 and 4.2: new mnemonic and transition, revision \"v3\".\n    68\t- yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md 325 to 340 and register.md 4.4: the `:define` opcode and lowering, revisions \"b2\" and \"r2\".\n    69\t- yin.vm.macro.md 4.1 `macro-of` and 4.2: reserved names are never macros; context admission rule; 2.2 definition key rule.\n    70\t- yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md 4.5 effect-from-profile paragraph and line 1831 \"free at that site\": the operator is syntax, not a free variable.\n    71\t- yin.vm.engine.md: dispatcher key guard. yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md: the revision entries.\n    72\t\n    73\t## 4. Sequencing\n    74\t\n    75\tAccept codex: land Rule R as its own gated change before the M2 commit, and drop the validator-only fallback. The evidence is the admission table above. The walker's datom and row loaders, the REPL, dao.await, and the expander's transformer VM all run programs that never see a linker validator, and a host effect can write the reserved key regardless. The fallback's soundness claim held only for linked images and only for the store, which is too narrow to call \"by construction.\" The two commits stay coherent as: Rule R with contracts and docs, gated on the tri-host lanes; then M2 with the round 3 and 4 filters deleted and fixtures flipped, gated once.\n    76\t\n    77\t## 5. Sound and complete for constant keys\n    78\t\n    79\tIt holds under one condition: **the image was admitted under a Rule R contract revision, and every path that can write its store passed a Rule R boundary.** Given that, every application whose operator is `yin/def` defines its literal key on every engine, the invocation position places the write after the value operand (linker.cljc:427 to 468), and no other write can target the name. The receiver's `:shadowed-free` case for `yin/def` disappears because `yin/def` is no longer a free name.\n    80\t\n    81\tResidual, all deferred and all over-retaining: `:vm/store-update` rows are not in the definition query and cannot enter an image anyway; export verification stays advisory; host-registry effects with computed keys stay incomplete-discovery. The missing stamp check at lowering is a defect, not deferred work.\n    82\t\n    83\t## 6. Completion criteria, per host (JVM, Node, Dart), all four backends\n    84\t\n    85\t1. Each row in the enforcement table has a test that fails before the change and refuses `:reserved-name` after, at the stated time (load or transition).\n    86\t2. A store seeded with a macro named `yin/def` makes `make-ctx` refuse, and a batch expanded against it is refused before any rewrite.\n    87\t3. A datom batch and a row set carrying a shared `[:variable yin/def]` row used once as a definition operator and once as a value are refused, proving the check is occurrence-aware.\n    88\t4. A host primitive returning a store-put effect keyed `yin/def` is refused at dispatch on all four engines.\n    89\t5. A supplied initial store, free env, or registry binding `yin/def` makes each constructor throw.\n    90\t6. Redefining `x` twice reads the second value; a definition inside a lambda body and inside an `:if` behaves as before.\n    91\t7. A lowered old-format image with `:load-free yin/def` is refused by validation; an AST request naming \"v2\" is `:contract-mismatch`; a request naming no contract is refused; a lifted continuation stamped \"v2\" is `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` at lowering.\n    92\t8. The three M2 fixtures and the alias fixture refuse `:reserved-name`; `(yin/def 'x 'yin/def)` links and defines `x` as the quoted symbol.\n    93\t9. Obligations of a definition-only module are empty on all four formats.\n    94\t10. A continuation parked inside a definition's value operand round-trips through UCF and completes the write; the frame carries a define marker, not a function object.\n    95\t11. Kondo, cljstyle, ASCII, and 80 columns clean.\n    96\t\n    97\tFiles read for this revision beyond the first round: macro.cljc context and store paths, ast_walker.cljc loaders and fast path, module.cljc, the three constructors' store options, ucf.cljc and ledger.cljc stamps, completion.cljc, the M2 format records, and every spec section codex cited. No test lane was run.\n     1\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-25 13:31:24 GMT\n     2\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-25 20:31:24 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n     3\t\n     4\tRule R is a sound direction for making definitions unshadowable, but Fable’s enforcement plan is not yet complete. This was a read-only review; no suites were rerun.\n     5\t\n     6\tFactual claims:\n     7\t\n     8\t- **PARTLY — `def` is already a special form.** The Yang frontend recognizes `def` before ordinary variable lookup, but lowers it to an ordinary application of `yin/def`; the VM does not yet preserve its special-form meaning (`src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:132-160`).\n     9\t- **TRUE — `yin/def` is an ordinary primitive today.** Its registry entry returns a `:vm/store-put` effect (`src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:194-197`).\n    10\t- **TRUE, narrowly — all four engines use the shared variable resolver.** The walker, semantic, stack, and register engines call `engine/resolve-var`, whose order is environment → store → primitives → modules (`src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:379`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:263-267`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc:386-390`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc:390-395`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:54-73`). Direct store instructions do not use it.\n    11\t- **FALSE as stated — only primitives raise effects, and all other store keys are syntactically visible.** Built-in store instructions write directly, and a composition-supplied host function can return an effect with a computed key; effect dispatch accepts it (`src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:419-438`, `src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:143-150`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:77-80`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:510-516`). The literal-key claim holds for the portable built-in store instructions, not every executable path.\n    12\t\n    13\tActionable findings:\n    14\t\n    15\tP1 | `collab/1790341000000-architect-yin-def-unshadowable.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:48` | Refusing newly harvested `yin/def` macros does not reject one already present in the expander’s incoming store. Expansion consults that store before expanding an application, so it can rewrite a definition before linker validation (`src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:973-980`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:1105-1109`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:1170-1172`). | Refuse a reserved macro binding at expander-context admission, including restored stores, and test a prior-batch binding.\n    16\t\n    17\tP1 | `collab/1790341000000-architect-yin-def-unshadowable.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:23-24` | “Every image reaching any engine has passed” linker validation is false: the walker also accepts datom input directly, and raw AST execution has variable, store-get, store-put, and store-update paths (`src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:709-721`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:377-438`). A row-local check also cannot classify a shared `yin/def` variable row by its use site; the linker’s AST scans are occurrence-path based (`src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc:289-319`). | Specify reserved-name checks at every direct admission/evaluation path and an occurrence-aware whole-tree check. Define which violations are rejected at load time versus transition time.\n    18\t\n    19\tP1 | `collab/1790341000000-architect-yin-def-unshadowable.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:14-15` | Removing the named primitive does not establish the proposed “store never contains `yin/def`” invariant: an allowed host callable can emit `{:effect :vm/store-put :key 'yin/def ...}`, and the central dispatcher currently accepts that key (`src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:188-196`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:510-516`). This would **not** shadow a properly implemented syntactic definition, but it invalidates Fable’s stronger store and discovery claims. | Guard the reserved key in effect dispatch, direct store operations, and supplied initial stores; retain incomplete-discovery treatment for dynamic host effects.\n    20\t\n    21\tP1 | `collab/1790341000000-architect-yin-def-unshadowable.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:38-46` | Fable explicitly calls for stack/register revisions but not new AST and semantic contracts. Rule R changes AST application interpretation and adds a semantic opcode; UCF requires a new revision for changed transitions or opcodes, while the linker still advertises `\"v2\"`, `\"b1\"`, and `\"r1\"` (`docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:245-274`, `docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:595-604`, `docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:630-699`). | Version all four execution contracts, update format records and manifest/derivation stamps, and require old-contract images to be refused or handled by an explicitly compatible loader.\n    22\t\n    23\tP2 | `collab/1790341000000-architect-yin-def-unshadowable.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:19` | “The symbol `yin/def` may occur only as operator” conflates a variable occurrence with quoted data. A literal symbol is data (`src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:140-143`); banning `(quote yin/def)` is unnecessary, while allowing it as a definition key is not. | State the rule by syntactic role: permit literal data, reject reserved binders and store keys, and validate the target key at any dynamic store-effect boundary.\n    24\t\n    25\t“Sound and complete for constant keys” is achievable **for admitted images under a single revised execution contract**: a valid `yin/def` application would always define its literal key, while the existing invocation position correctly places the write after its value operand (`src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc:427-468`). It is not established by the present proposal across macro ingress, direct execution, and old-contract or foreign engines.\n    26\t\n    27\tA dedicated `:vm/define` node is not inherently contrary to “derive, don’t persist”: it would encode a distinct operation once, but would migrate the row grammar and content hashes (`src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:795-807`). Keeping the current application shape is reasonable if one shared recognizer governs validation and lowering and the walker has matching transition tests. Fable is right to reject primitives-first lookup: it would leave `yin/def` first-class and aliasable. Once Rule R is fully enforced, the round-3/4 shadow filters and `:yin-def?` bookkeeping can go (`src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc:401-424`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc:474-478`); constant-key recognition, invocation positioning, dominance, and the earlier fetch-bound fixes must stay (`src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc:301-319`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc:427-468`).\n    28\t\n    29\tLand a complete, independently gated Rule R change before M2 if two commits can each remain coherent. Do **not** use Fable’s validator-only M2 fallback: direct execution and dynamic host effects remain outside that proof (`collab/1790341000000-architect-yin-def-unshadowable.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:69-75`). Update the contradictions Fable identified in UCF and the linker, and also the macro contract’s stored-macro lookup, the semantic/stack/register instruction contracts, and code-as-tuples’ assertion that the `yin/def` operator is free (`docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md:373-396`, `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:231-257`, `docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md:325-340`, `docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:369-385`, `docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1828-1832`). Store-update discovery may remain deferred; the admission, macro, and contract gaps above cannot.\n    30\t\n    31\tVerdict: REQUEST CHANGES\n    32\tSign-off: DENIED\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
