n/canonical-value-table`: :ratio and :bigint are their\n    99\t   own classes here, not excluded, and :int64 is named :long (S2's own\n   100\t   wording) with no NFC or integral-double folding anywhere in this\n   101\t   table's encoding.\"\n   102\t  [:nil :bool :long :double :ratio :bigint :char :string :keyword :symbol\n   103\t   :vector :list :map :set])\n   104\t\n   105\t\n   305\t   the JVM, distinct from `int?` (which is also true for both on the\n   306\t   JVM, so it cannot be used to tell them apart). `yin.vm.debruijn/\n   307\t   numeric-class` -- the existing repo pattern for cross-host numeric\n   308\t   classification -- does not attempt bigint detection on :cljd either\n   309\t   (its :cljd branch falls straight to double-class for anything\n   310\t   `int?` does not claim); this dimension matches that absence rather\n   311\t   than guess an unverified ClojureDart class name that could either\n   312\t   silently misclassify or fail to compile. A Dart or JS bigint value is\n   313\t   refused with :unsupported-value rather than encoded.\"\n   314\t  #_{:clj-kondo/ignore [:unused-binding]}\n   315\t  [v]\n   316\t  #?(:cljd false\n   317\t     :cljs false\n   318\t     :clj (or (instance? clojure.lang.BigInt v) (instance? java.math.BigInteger v))))\n   319\t\n   320\t\n   321\t(defn- host-long?\n   322\t  \"A JVM boxed integral type; a CLJS safe integer (S2 item 4's own\n   323\t   wording); a Dart native int (`int?`), the same test `yin.vm.debruijn/\n   324\t   numeric-class` already uses for this host.\"\n   325\t  [v]\n   326\t  #?(:clj (or (instance? Long v) (instance? Integer v) (instance? Short v) (instance? Byte v))\n   327\t     :cljs (and (number? v) (js/Number.isSafeInteger v))\n   328\t     :cljd (int? v)))\n   329\t\n   330\t\n   331\t(defn- host-double?\n   332\t  \"On the JVM, exactly `java.lang.Double` -- not 'every remaining number':\n   333\t   a JVM BigDecimal or Float is a distinct type this dimension's 14-class\n   334\t   domain does not declare, and folding either into :double would hash\n   335\t   its value at whatever precision `(double v)` happens to produce\n   336\t   instead of refusing it, exactly the silent canonicalization S2\n   337\t   forbids. On CLJS and CLJD, every remaining number: not a ratio, not a\n   338\t   bigint, not a long by this host's own rule -- CLJS has no BigDecimal/\n   339\t   Float distinction from a plain number to lose, so the complement rule\n   340\t   stays correct there.\"\n   341\t  [v]\n   342\t  #?(:clj (instance? Double v)\n   343\t     :default (and (number? v) (not (host-long? v)) (not (host-ratio? v)) (not (host-bigint? v)))))\n   344\t\n   345\t\n   346\t(defn scalar-class\n   347\t  \"Classify `v` under this dimension's own S2 scalar domain: the class\n   348\t   keyword when `v` is representable, nil when it is not. Recursive over\n   349\t   vectors, lists, maps, and sets -- a collection classifies only when\n   350\t   every element does. No normalization and no folding of any kind\n    65\t;; own positional shape: an ordered vector of `[attribute kind]` pairs.\n    66\t\n    67\t(def opcode-table\n    68\t  \"Design section 4.4's register instruction set, in positional operand-\n    69\t   table form.\"\n    70\t  {:const [[:yin.debruijn.register/rd :reg]\n    71\t           [:yin.debruijn.register/value :data]]\n    72\t\n    73\t   :load-bound [[:yin.debruijn.register/rd :reg]\n    74\t                [:yin.debruijn.register/depth :uint]\n    75\t                [:yin.debruijn.register/position :uint]]\n    76\t\n    77\t   :load-free [[:yin.debruijn.register/rd :reg]\n    78\t               [:yin.debruijn.register/name :sym]]\n    79\t\n    80\t   :closure [[:yin.debruijn.register/rd :reg]\n    81\t             [:yin.debruijn.register/arity :uint]\n    82\t             [:yin.debruijn.register/body-pc :pc]]\n    83\t\n    84\t   :move [[:yin.debruijn.register/rd :reg]\n    85\t          [:yin.debruijn.register/rs :reg]]\n    86\t\n    87\t   :call [[:yin.debruijn.register/rd :reg]\n    88\t          [:yin.debruijn.register/fn-reg :reg]\n    89\t          [:yin.debruijn.register/arg-regs :regs]\n    90\t          [:yin.debruijn.register/tail? :bool]\n    91\t          [:yin.debruijn.register/live :data]]\n    92\t\n    93\t   :branch-false [[:yin.debruijn.register/cond-reg :reg]\n    94\t                  [:yin.debruijn.register/target :pc]]\n    95\t\n    96\t   :jump [[:yin.debruijn.register/target :pc]]\n    97\t\n    98\t   :return [[:yin.debruijn.register/value-reg :reg]]\n    99\t\n   100\t   :halt [[:yin.debruijn.register/value-reg :reg]]\n   101\t\n   102\t   :store-get [[:yin.debruijn.register/rd :reg]\n   103\t               [:yin.debruijn.register/key :data]]\n   104\t\n   105\t   :store-put [[:yin.debruijn.register/rd :reg]\n   350\t              (fn [[in out changed?] pc]\n   351\t                (let [t (nth instructions pc)\n   352\t                      new-out (reduce (fn [s s-pc] (into s (nth in s-pc)))\n   353\t                                      (sorted-set)\n   354\t                                      (successors-of instructions pc))\n   355\t                      new-in (into (use-of t) (reduce disj new-out (def-of t)))]\n   356\t                  [(assoc in pc new-in) (assoc out pc new-out)\n   357\t                   (or changed? (not= new-in (nth in pc))\n   358\t                       (not= new-out (nth out pc)))]))\n   359\t              [live-in live-out false]\n   360\t              (rseq pcs))]\n   361\t        (if changed?\n   362\t          (recur live-in' live-out')\n   363\t          (into {}\n   364\t                (keep (fn [pc]\n   365\t                        (let [t (nth instructions pc)\n   366\t                              op (nth t 0)]\n   367\t                          (when (boundary-opcodes op)\n   368\t                            [pc (vec (disj (nth live-out' pc) (nth t 1)))]))))\n   369\t                pcs))))))\n   370\t\n   371\t\n   372\t;; =============================================================================\n   373\t;; The validator (design section 3, section 4.2-4.3)\n   374\t;; =============================================================================\n   375\t;; `register-image-defect`: the sole admitter of a register image on this\n   376\t;; path (mirroring B1's `image-defect`). Checks generic tuple shape first\n   377\t;; (`nonempty`, `mnemonic-rule`, `arity-rule`, `operand-kind-rule`,\n   378\t;; `target-bounds-rule` -- all mirroring B1's own rules of the same name,\n   379\t;; over this dimension's own opcode table), then the checks new to this\n   380\t;; format: `body-scope-rule` (every body range is well formed, every\n   381\t;; closure's body pc names a real body of matching arity), `jump-scope-\n   382\t;; rule` (every `:jump`/`:branch-false` target lies within its own\n   383\t;; instruction's body, not merely within `target-bounds-rule`'s whole-\n   384\t;; vector bound) and `register-bounds-rule` (every register operand is\n   385\t;; within its own body's declared register count), the four live-set\n   386\t;; rules of design section 4.5 (`live-shape-rule`, `live-bounds-rule`,\n   387\t;; `live-tail-rule`, `live-exact-rule`), and `terminator-rule` (every\n   388\t;; body ends `:return`, the main body `:halt`).\n   389\t\n   390\t(defn- tuple-defect\n   391\t  [rule pc]\n   392\t  {:rule rule, :pc pc})\n   393\t\n   394\t\n   395\t(defn- body-defect\n   396\t  [rule body-index]\n   397\t  {:rule rule, :body body-index})\n   398\t\n   399\t\n   400\t(defn- nonempty\n   520\t   `yin.vm.code` reads one.\"\n   521\t  [datoms]\n   522\t  (reduce (fn [[order attrs] [e a v]]\n   523\t            [(if (contains? attrs e) order (conj order e))\n   524\t             (assoc-in attrs [e a] v)])\n   525\t          [[] {}]\n   526\t          datoms))\n   527\t\n   528\t\n   529\t(defn load-image\n   530\t  \"Decode one `:yin.code/*` batch into an executable image.\n   531\t\n   532\t   Returns `{:segment id, :length n, :code instructions}` where `instructions`\n   533\t   is a vector indexed by pc of decoded `[opcode & operands]` vectors with\n   534\t   every `:yin.code/target` and `:yin.code/body` ref resolved to a pc integer\n   535\t   of the same segment, plus `:address` when the segment entity carries\n   536\t   `:yin.code/hash` (§2.2) — a claim checked here: the canonical vector the\n   537\t   batch reconstructs to must hash to it (UCF §7.3.4's content-integrity\n   538\t   check, its `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` outcome), so a false claim never\n   539\t   reaches the alias column. Throws a load error naming the entity and rule\n   540\t   when the batch is not well formed (§2.6); the loader is total over the\n   541\t   outcomes of its inputs and does not guess.\"\n   542\t  [datoms]\n   543\t  (let [defect (code/well-formed? datoms)]\n   544\t    (when defect\n   545\t      (throw (ex-info (str \"Cannot load segment: \" (name (:rule defect))\n   546\t                           \" (entity \" (:entity defect) \")\")\n   547\t                      {:defect defect})))\n   548\t    (let [[order attrs] (index-batch datoms)\n   549\t          segments (filterv #(= :segment (get-in attrs [% :yin.code/type]))\n   550\t                            order)\n   551\t          seg (first segments)\n   552\t          structural [:yin.code/segment :yin.code/pc :yin.code/op]\n   553\t          instructions (filterv (fn [e]\n   554\t                                  (and (not= seg e)\n   555\t                                       (some #(contains? (get attrs e) %)\n   705\t      [(:tailcall vm/opcode-table) (nth t 1)]\n   706\t      [(:call vm/opcode-table) (nth t 1)])\n   707\t    (let [mnem (nth t 0)\n   708\t          opcode (or (get vm/opcode-table (get mnemonic-aliases mnem mnem))\n   709\t                     (throw (ex-info \"Mnemonic has no opcode\" {:op mnem})))]\n   710\t      (into [opcode] (rest t)))))\n   711\t\n   712\t\n   713\t(defn load-vector\n   714\t  \"Load one canonical instruction vector (UCF §7.3.2) by the direct path\n   715\t   (§7.1): validate it against §7.5 — a defect throws naming the pc — then\n   716\t   decode the positional operands into the same image `load-image` builds\n   717\t   from the projected batch, and register it under `:code` with the\n   718\t   vector's address `(jing/segment-key v)` in the alias column.\n   719\t\n   720\t   A vector claims no local id, so one is minted below the loaded floor\n   721\t   (`vm/loaded-code-floor`), as `ast-loader` mints for lowered AST batches;\n   722\t   an address already aliased reloads under its id, which `store-image`\n   723\t   accepts as the identical image. Options:\n   724\t     :id claim this local segment id instead of minting one. The §3.1 rule\n   725\t          applies: an id already holding a different image is a load error,\n   726\t          an identical reload is accepted. Ignored when the address is\n   727\t          already aliased.\"\n   728\t  ([vm v] (load-vector vm v {}))\n   729\t  ([vm v opts]\n   730\t   (when-let [defect (code/well-formed-vector? v)]\n   731\t     (throw (ex-info (str \"Cannot load vector: \" (name (:rule defect))\n   732\t                          \" (pc \" (:pc defect) \")\")\n   733\t                     {:defect defect})))\n   734\t   (let [address (jing/segment-key v)\n   735\t         aliases (:code-aliases vm)\n   736\t         seg (or (get aliases address)\n   737\t                 (:id opts)\n   738\t                 (dec (vm/loaded-code-floor (:code vm))))\n   739\t         image {:segment seg,\n   740\t                :length (count v),\n   741\t                :code (mapv decode-tuple v),\n   742\t                :address address}]\n   743\t     (assoc vm\n   744\t            :program seg\n   745\t            :code (store-image (:code vm) image)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
