design/yin.vm.linker.md:2452:| binding was lost on export; the   | lifts as `:yin.k/store` beside    |\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2454:|                                   | `:yin.k/store-of` the manifest    |\n  1328\t    that dependency's store instance as it stands in the child's\n  1329\t    state at halt -- including mutations the child made through the\n  1330\t    dependency's own closures, which a snapshot of the child's store\n  1331\t    alone would not carry. A `:store-of` with no snapshot is a lift\n  1332\t    refusal, `:yin.k/non-portable` with\n  1333\t    `:yin.k/kind :missing-module-store`. Each snapshot encodes as a\n  1334\t    `:yin.k/store` slice (UCF 7.6.2); whatever that grammar refuses\n  1335\t    (a host object in the store; engine resources cannot be, by the\n  1336\t    private-resource rule below) refuses the lift and the install.\n  1337\t  - *Marker.* Every closure marker names the store its body resolves\n  1338\t    against, `:yin.k/store-of m` with `m` the module's manifest\n  1339\t    address -- an amendment to UCF section 7.5.1 listed in section\n  1340\t    11, item 12. The snapshots travel once per slice, not per\n  1341\t    closure.\n  1342\t  - *Lower.* Each receiving task instantiates every snapshot into\n  1343\t    `:module-stores {m {sym value}}` in its own state, one instance\n  1344\t    per module: a store the task already holds stands as\n  1345\t    instantiated -- first link wins, and a later slice never\n  1346\t    overwrites a live instance -- while snapshots it lacks are\n  1347\t    instantiated fresh, so a task sees exactly one store per module.\n  1348\t    Nothing is written into any task's ambient store: this is\n  1349\t    7.6.2's \"there is no merge\" rule at the module grain -- a\n  1350\t    receiver binding of the same name can neither supply nor shadow\n  1351\t    the module's value.\n  1352\t  - *Run time.* While a closure carrying `:store-of m` executes, the\n  1353\t    module store of m is its **active store**: free reads resolve\n  1354\t    through it first and the receiver's free env, primitives, and\n  1355\t    registry second; the ambient store does not participate. Direct\n  1356\t    store instructions route by the same context -- `:store-put`\n  1357\t    (and the `:vm/store-put` effect) writes the active module\n  1358\t    store, and `:store-get` reads it with **no ambient fallback**:\n  1359\t    a key the module store lacks is absent, exactly as a top-level\n  1360\t    read of an absent key is; engine resources are unreachable\n  1361\t    either way, by the private-resource rule below. The context\n  1362\t    threads through calls: a frame records the caller's active\n  1363\t    `:store-of` (or none), applying a closure makes that closure's\n  1364\t    `:store-of` active, and returning restores the frame's, so a\n  1365\t    module closure calling another module's closure routes each\n  1366\t    body's reads and writes to its own module. A tail call that\n  1367\t    empties the continuation -- the closure's body was the whole\n  1368\t    computation -- clears the active context with the last frame,\n  1369\t    and the VM admits new top-level input only with no active\n  1370\t    context, which `yin.repl`'s append asserts, so input after a\n   850\t\n   851\tThe reference machine's scheduler state does not live in the store, and the\n   852\tfirst draft transported none of it. Both omissions are defects: the\n   853\tmachine's fresh identifiers come from `:id-counter`, and `:resume` resolves\n   854\tits operand against the `:parked` map — a fresh receiver could reuse an\n   855\texisting generated identifier, or fail to resolve a parked continuation a\n   856\tmigrated segment is about to name (`engine/park-continuation`,\n   857\t`engine/resume-continuation`, §3.5). UCF therefore states the unit and\n   858\tcarries the state:\n   859\t\n   860\t- **The unit is the whole blocked machine**: registers (the frame), the\n   861\t  wait-set (as pending variants, §7.4.3), the referenced `:parked` records,\n   862\t  and the fresh-name counter. The ready-queue must be empty at lift\n   863\t  (`:yin.k/not-quiescent` otherwise, §7.4.1) — a task with queued runnable\n   864\t  work has a next configuration that is not the parked one.\n   865\t- **Fresh-name state travels.** `:yin.k/scheduler {:yin.k/id-counter n\n   866\t  …}` carries the counter verbatim; the lowered machine adopts\n   867\t  `max(local, carried)` so generated ids never repeat, and ids already\n   868\t  minted (gensym results in env, stack, or store) travel as ordinary values,\n   869\t  unaffected by the counter.\n   870\t- **Referenced parked records travel.** The fixed point\n   871\t  of §7.6.1 collects every `:resume` operand in reachable code and every\n   872\t  `[:parked id]` value from reachable environments. Each must resolve\n   873\t  within this task to a parked record carried under\n   874\t  `:yin.k/scheduler :yin.k/parked {pid → frame}`. A missing id referenced\n   875\t  as a code operand is reported as an unsupplied requirement\n   876\t  (`:yin.k/unsatisfied`). A missing id referenced by an active value\n   877\t  refuses the lift (`:yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:foreign-parked-ref`),\n   878\t  as it represents corrupted state. Parked records not named by the\n   879\t  reachable graph do not travel.\n   880\t\n   881\t### 7.6.4 Modules\n   882\t\n   883\t`:yin.k/modules` names each module by the content address of its published\n   884\tmanifest, on the model of `dao.space.index/publish-index!` (`dao.jing.md`,\n   885\t*Publication from an agent*). A module is satisfied when the resumer holds a\n   886\tmodule registry entry (`yin.vm.module` — a value, supplied by the\n   887\tcomposition) whose manifest address is equal; it is not satisfied by a\n   888\tsame-named module of different content. Module *code* is segments and is\n   889\tcovered by `:yin.k/segments` and the manifest's own addressing; the manifest\n   890\tpins the effect handlers, the exported primitive profiles (§7.5.2), and —\n   891\tper §7.6.1 — the store footprint the fixed point needs to claim\n   892\t`:complete`.\n   893\t\n   894\t### 7.6.5 The satisfaction check\n   895\t\n  1030\tappend is the effect.\n  1031\t\n  1032\tUCF therefore defines an explicit **exporting** transition that precedes\n  1033\tany publication, and the lift driver performs it as one step over the\n  1034\tmachine value:\n  1035\t\n  1036\t1. **Quiesce**: the ready-queue must be empty (§7.6.3).\n  1037\t2. **Detach the wait set**: every wait entry moves from the machine's\n  1038\t   wait-set into the export record. The machine's wait-set is now empty,\n  1039\t   so *there is nothing to poll* — no poll, no append, no effect. The\n  1040\t   entries are not lost: the export record retains them verbatim; they are\n  1041\t   the source of the `:yin.k/pending` variants of §7.4.3.\n  1042\t3. **Neutralize control**: the record's control and K are already absent\n  1043\t   (the machine is parked or blocked); direct `:resume` resolves against\n  1044\t   `:parked`, and the parked records referenced by reachable code travel\n  1045\t   inside the export record — a local `:resume` against them is refused\n  1046\t   while exporting (the restore function observes the exporting machine\n  1047\t   and declines), because the parked id now names a checkpoint that may be\n  1048\t   granted elsewhere.\n  1049\t4. **Publish, then offer**: append segments and the value body; record\n  1050\t   `:yin.k/offered` on the arbitration medium. Only now do readers exist,\n  1051\t   and the source was fenced before any of them did.\n  1052\t\n  1053\tThe exporting machine is pure data with an empty wait-set and a retained\n  1054\texport record — no engine change, no callback, no timer; the drivers of the\n  1055\tworld observe the state as they observe `ready-for-ingress?` (§3.1), and a\n  1056\tdriver that polls an exporting machine's wait set anyway is a host assembly\n  1057\tdefect of the same kind as wiring a refusing deposit destination.\n  1058\t\n  1059\t**Failure recovery and idempotent retry.** If publication fails — a full\n  1060\tcarrier, an unreachable arbitration medium — the machine stays exporting\n  1061\tand the composition retries; a retry re-encodes or reuses the encoded value\n  1062\tand **reuses the occurrence** (§7.3.4), so no retry mints a second runnable\n  1063\tcheckpoint. Abandoning the export reinstates the export record into the\n  1064\tmachine (wait entries return to the wait-set, parked records to `:parked`)\n  1065\tand the task resumes as a purely local one — the sole paths back to local\n  1066\texecution are *abort export* (before the offer is recorded) and *a grant to\n  1067\tthe emitter itself* (after); there is no third.\n  1068\t\n  1069\t### 7.7.5 Fencing effects: epochs, operation ids, and what exactly-once costs\n  1070\t\n  1071\t`dao.lease.md` states it plainly: **the absence of a `:lapsed` fact is\n  1072\tnever evidence of tenure.** The first draft's fencing — stamp effects with\n  1073\tthe lease id, let consumers consult the medium and drop effects whose\n  1074\tincarnation has a `:lapsed` record — fails in every direction the review\n  1075\tnamed: a partitioned consumer accepts stale effects (it cannot see the\n   135\t  ([segment] (create-vm segment {}))\n   136\t  ([segment opts]\n   137\t   (let [base (vm/empty-state\n   138\t                (assoc (select-keys opts [:modules :make-stream :call-in\n   139\t                                          :call-out :call-capacity])\n   140\t                       :primitives (or (:primitives opts) {})))]\n   141\t     (-> (map->DebruijnVM\n   142\t           {:segment [],\n   143\t            :hash nil,\n   144\t            :pc 0,\n   145\t            :frames [],\n   146\t            :free-env (or (:free-env opts) {}),\n   147\t            :stack [],\n   148\t            :continuation [],\n   149\t        