ursor out of the store by `waitset-resolver` and\n   308\t   polled with a synchronous `next` or `append!` — resolution happens per\n   309\t   poll, so the stored entry itself never carries a handle. There is no\n   310\t   transport-local waking to fall back from: this is the only mechanism.\n   311\t\n   312\t   Entries are polled one at a time in wait-set order, and a woken reader's\n   313\t   successor cursor is written back to the store before the next entry is\n   314\t   resolved. Waiters sharing a cursor-ref therefore read distinct values —\n   315\t   the value at the cursor wakes the first, its successor wakes the next —\n   316\t   instead of every waiter reading the value at the shared pre-poll\n   317\t   cursor. An entry that stays waiting is retained in its stored,\n   318\t   resource-id form.\n   319\t\n   320\t   Each woken result's `:status` is stamped onto its ready entry, where\n   321\t   `terminal-resume-outcome` reads it: an outcome the immediate operation\n   322\t   raises as an error fails the same way when the resume pops it — and a\n   323\t   waitset diagnostic, an entry the sweep could not poll at all, raises\n   324\t   there too, before any continuation is restored.\"\n   325\t  [state]\n   326\t  (let [wait-set (:wait-set state)]\n   327\t    (if (empty? wait-set)\n   328\t      state\n   329\t      (let [{:keys [woken store], :as result}\n   330\t            (waitset/check {:waiting wait-set} waitset-resolver (:store state))\n   331\t            v (assoc state\n   332\t                     :store store\n404:(§7.6.3). Lifting additionally requires the machine's ready-queue to be\n862:  and the fresh-name counter. The ready-queue must be empty at lift\n1036:1. **Quiesce**: the ready-queue must be empty (§7.6.3).\n1180:   ready-queue is empty, else `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.\n1246:| `:yin.k/not-quiescent` | lift | the ready-queue depth |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
