efect as\n   375\t   `{:rule r :pc p}` — `:nonempty`, `:mnemonic`, `:arity`,\n   376\t   `:operand-kind`, `:saturation`, `:target-bounds`, `:terminator`, or\n   377\t   `:argc` — with p the offending pc (`:nonempty` names pc 0, where the\n   378\t   missing first instruction belongs). Rules run in §7.5's order, each\n   379\t   assuming the earlier ones held. §2.6 rules 1–4 are satisfied by the\n   380\t   vector form by construction (pc is the index); rules 5–6 and the UCF\n   381\t   §7.3.4 checks are the eight here. The address check — a vector hashing\n   382\t   to the address it claims — is not a structural rule and is not made\n   383\t   here: a correct hash is never structural validation.\"\n   384\t  [v]\n   385\t  (some #(% v) vector-rules))\n   386\t\n   387\t\n   388\t;; =============================================================================\n   389\t;; Segment rows (§6.2)\n   390\t;; =============================================================================\n   540\t   when the batch is not well formed (§2.6); the loader is total over the\n   541\t   outcomes of its inputs and does not guess.\"\n   542\t  [datoms]\n   543\t  (let [defect (code/well-formed? datoms)]\n   544\t    (when defect\n   545\t      (throw (ex-info (str \"Cannot load segment: \" (name (:rule defect))\n   546\t                           \" (entity \" (:entity defect) \")\")\n   547\t                      {:defect defect})))\n   548\t    (let [[order attrs] (index-batch datoms)\n   549\t          segments (filterv #(= :segment (get-in attrs [% :yin.code/type]))\n   550\t                            order)\n   551\t          seg (first segments)\n   552\t          structural [:yin.code/segment :yin.code/pc :yin.code/op]\n   553\t          instructions (filterv (fn [e]\n   554\t                                  (and (not= seg e)\n   555\t                                       (some #(contains? (get attrs e) %)\n   670\t(defn vm-load-program\n   671\t  \"Load one `:yin.code/*`