{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/code.cljc | sed -n '48,100p;100,125p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc | sed -n '255,267p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | sed -n '405,430p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '1180,1226p'\"","aggregated_output":"    48\t(defn- defect\n    49\t  [rule entity]\n    50\t  {:rule rule, :entity entity})\n    51\t\n    52\t\n    53\t(defn- index-batch\n    54\t  \"Entity ids in order of first appearance, and each entity's attribute map.\n    55\t   A repeated single-valued attribute keeps its last value, as\n    56\t   `yin.vm/index-datoms` reads one.\"\n    57\t  [datoms]\n    58\t  (reduce (fn [[order attrs] [e a v]]\n    59\t            [(if (contains? attrs e) order (conj order e))\n    60\t             (assoc-in attrs [e a] v)])\n    61\t          [[] {}]\n    62\t          datoms))\n    63\t\n    64\t\n    65\t;; Each rule below takes the indexed batch and returns a defect or nil. Rules\n    66\t;; run in order and the first defect wins, so a rule may assume every earlier\n    67\t;; one held.\n    68\t\n    69\t(defn- one-segment\n    70\t  \"Rule 1. A batch with no segment entity names no entity; a batch with\n    71\t   several names the second.\"\n    72\t  [{:keys [segments]}]\n    73\t  (when-not (= 1 (count segments))\n    74\t    (defect :one-segment (second segments))))\n    75\t\n    76\t\n    77\t(defn- instruction-shape\n    78\t  \"§2.3: every instruction names this segment and carries a §2.4 op. The\n    79\t   later rules read both, so a batch that lacks them cannot be judged by them.\"\n    80\t  [{:keys [seg attrs instructions]}]\n    81\t  (some (fn [e]\n    82\t          (let [ia (get attrs e)]\n    83\t            (when-not (and (= seg (:yin.code/segment ia))\n    84\t                           (contains? mnemonics (:yin.code/op ia)))\n    85\t              (defect :instruction-shape e))))\n    86\t        instructions))\n    87\t\n    88\t\n    89\t(defn- dense-pcs\n    90\t  \"Rule 2. The first instruction whose pc is missing, out of range, or\n    91\t   already taken is named; a count short of the length names the segment.\"\n    92\t  [{:keys [seg attrs instructions]}]\n    93\t  (let [length (get-in attrs [seg :yin.code/length])]\n    94\t    (if-not (count? length)\n    95\t      (defect :dense-pcs seg)\n    96\t      (loop [es instructions\n    97\t             seen #{}]\n    98\t        (if-let [[e & more] (seq es)]\n    99\t          (let [pc (get-in attrs [e :yin.code/pc])]\n   100\t            (if (and (count? pc) (< pc length) (not (contains? seen pc)))\n   100\t            (if (and (count? pc) (< pc length) (not (contains? seen pc)))\n   101\t              (recur more (conj seen pc))\n   102\t              (defect :dense-pcs e)))\n   103\t          (when-not (= length (count seen))\n   104\t            (defect :dense-pcs seg)))))))\n   105\t\n   106\t\n   107\t(defn- sorted-by-pc\n   108\t  \"Rule 3. The pcs are a permutation of 0 .. length-1 by rule 2, so the batch\n   109\t   is sorted exactly when each instruction's pc is its position among the\n   110\t   instructions.\"\n   111\t  [{:keys [attrs instructions]}]\n   112\t  (some (fn [[i e]]\n   113\t          (when-not (= i (get-in attrs [e :yin.code/pc]))\n   114\t            (defect :sorted-by-pc e)))\n   115\t        (map-indexed vector instructions)))\n   116\t\n   117\t\n   118\t(defn- dangling-target\n   119\t  \"Rule 4. A ref that is present must name an instruction of this batch, and\n   120\t   a `:jump`, `:branch-false`, or `:closure` without its ref resolves nowhere.\"\n   121\t  [{:keys [attrs instructions]}]\n   122\t  (let [instruction? (set instructions)]\n   123\t    (some (fn [e]\n   124\t            (let [ia (get attrs e)\n   125\t                  required (get required-ref (:yin.code/op ia))\n   255\t        (if (and fuel (zero? fuel))\n   256\t          (put-registers vm seg pc val St E K)\n   257\t          (let [inst (nth (:code image) pc)\n   258\t                op (nth inst 0)]\n   259\t            (case op\n   260\t              ;; :const — val ← literal (opcode 1, :literal)\n   261\t              1 (recur seg (inc pc) (nth inst 1) St E K vm image\n   262\t                       (and fuel (dec fuel)))\n   263\t              ;; :var — val ← resolve(E, S, prims, modules, name) (2, :load-var)\n   264\t              2 (recur seg (inc pc)\n   265\t                       (engine/resolve-var E (:store vm) (:primitives vm)\n   266\t                                           (:modules vm) (nth inst 1))\n   267\t                       St E K vm image (and fuel (dec fuel)))\n   405\t  \"Pop first entry from the ready-queue, merge store-updates, and restore\n   406\t   VM-specific context.\n   407\t\n   408\t   Restoration is dispatched here rather than carried on the entry, so a\n   409\t   ready entry holds registers, ids, and values — never a closure. The\n   410\t   terminal-outcome check runs before the restore: a woken retry that ended\n   411\t   in an outcome the immediate operation raises as an error must fail the\n   412\t   same way here, not reach Yin code as a value.\n   413\t\n   414\t   `restore-fn` is called with three arguments, `base entry val`, where\n   415\t   `val` is the woken `:value` — the same signature `resume-continuation`\n   416\t   uses, so one restore serves both engine call sites\n   417\t   (`yin.vm.engine.md` §7, edit 2).\"\n   418\t  [state restore-fn]\n   419\t  (let [run-queue (or (:ready-queue state) [])]\n   420\t    (when (seq run-queue)\n   421\t      (let [entry (first run-queue)\n   422\t            rest-queue (subvec run-queue 1)\n   423\t            base (assoc state\n   424\t                        :ready-queue rest-queue\n   425\t                        :store (merge (:store state) (:store-updates entry))\n   426\t                        :blocked? false\n   427\t                        :halted? false)]\n   428\t        (if-let [terminal (terminal-resume-outcome entry)]\n   429\t          (throw-terminal-resume! entry terminal)\n   430\t          (restore-fn base entry (:value entry)))))))\n  1180\t   ready-queue is empty, else `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.\n  1181\t2. **Enter exporting** (§7.7.4): detach the wait set into the export\n  1182\t   record, neutralize direct `:resume`. From here the source is fenced; no\n  1183\t   poll, retry, or local resume can perform an effect.\n  1184\t3. `lift(export-record)`: confirm the record is at a safepoint (§7.4.1),\n  1185\t   else `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; canonicalize every referenced segment and\n  1186\t   compute its address (§7.3.2), else the refusal; encode (§7.5), else the\n  1187\t   refusal; compute `:yin.k/requires` and `:yin.k/store` by the fixed point\n  1188\t   (§7.6), recording the discovery status; stamp the contract (§7.3.3); on\n  1189\t   first export, mint the occurrence; mint `:yin.k/id`.\n  1190\t4. Append the canonical instruction vectors of every required segment to a\n  1191\t   `dao.jing` intake pool (the payloads *are* the vectors, §7.3.4), then\n  1192\t   the id-less value body to the carrier medium; observe `:dao.stream/ok`\n  1193\t   on each. `:yin.k/carried` may then be empty. Retry of any failed append\n  1194\t   reuses the occurrence (§7.7.4).\n  1195\t5. Under `:exclusive`, append `:yin.k/offered` to the arbitration medium.\n  1196\t   The export record stays for recovery; the emitter is now a candidate\n  1197\t   like any other, and wakes only on custody (a grant to itself), never on\n  1198\t   data.\n  1199\t\n  1200\t**Lower** (resumer side):\n  1201\t\n  1202\t1. Read the value; check `:yin.k/contract`, else `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`.\n  1203\t2. Verify `:yin.k/id` by re-hashing the body, else `:yin.k/hash-mismatch`.\n  1204\t3. Decode against the §7.5.1 grammar with full structural validation, else\n  1205\t   `:yin.k/undecodable` — a correct hash is not structural validation, and\n  1206\t   both checks always run.\n  1207\t4. Evaluate `:yin.k/requires` (§7.6.5); fetch what may be fetched (segments\n  1208\t   by address, verified by re-canonicalization); else\n  1209\t   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with the discovery status.\n  1210\t5. Under `:exclusive`, attach to the arbitration medium (an attachment\n  1211\t   failure here is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming it — the value declares its\n  1212\t   authority, §7.7.3), propose, then observe the grant, else\n  1213\t   `:yin.k/not-holder` with the lease state observed; while a proposal is\n  1214\t   unanswered the driver's step answers `:yin.k/awaiting-grant` — a\n  1215\t   grantor owes no answer and no bound applies (`dao.lease.md`,\n  1216\t   *Authority*).\n  1217\t6. Load every required segment by address into the per-stamp index,\n  1218\t   verifying each (§7.3.4).\n  1219\t7. **Restore into an isolated store** (§7.6.2): the slice, the remapped\n  1220\t   cells, the resumer's FFI pair. Attach to every stream descriptor in the\n  1221\t   frame, pending waits, and cells (§7.4.3); an attachment `not-found` is\n  1222\t   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream. **Any failure from this step\n  1223\t   onward — after custody was acquired — releases first**: the resumer\n  1224\t   appends `:dao.lease/released`, the occurrence returns to offered, and\n  1225\t   the failure outcome is returned. A resumer that dies without releasing\n  1226\t   is recovered by the lease's own lapse; nothing else is owed.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
