{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '795,831p;350,369p'; rg -n 'contract.*optional|optional.*contract|:yin.link/contract.*optional' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md\"","aggregated_output":"   350\t                 name bound in free env or store    -> :shadowed-free\n   351\t6. return the verified image as data\n   352\t```\n   353\t\n   354\tStep by step, for all four formats:\n   355\t\n   356\t0. **Admit the request.** Before any lookup or fetch, the linker checks\n   357\t   the request's shape against the closed key set of section 6.3\n   358\t   (`:invalid-request`), that it holds a format record for the requested\n   359\t   format (`:unsupported-format`), and that the request's\n   360\t   `:yin.link/contract`, when present, equals the record's `:contract`\n   361\t   (`:contract-mismatch`). The contract check runs here, ahead of step\n   362\t   2's row-local grammar check, so no validator ever runs under the\n   363\t   wrong table; revision r2 placed it in step 4, after row-local\n   364\t   validation had already run, which was an ordering defect. For a\n   365\t   manifest-delivered image the same check runs again against the\n   366\t   manifest's per-format entry under `:yin.module/contracts`\n   367\t   immediately after the manifest is verified and before any derivation\n   368\t   or image is fetched (section 8.1). The manifest's own schema version\n   369\t   is a different thing and is checked by the manifest validator.\n   795\talready has, the portable linker is a pure function over explicit state.\n   796\t\n   797\t```clojure\n   798\t(link-state opts)               ; -> state; no socket, atom, or scheduler\n   799\t(request-link state request)    ; -> [state link-id]\n   800\t(step state budget)             ; -> {:state s :completions [...]\n   801\t                                ;     :diagnostics [...]}\n   802\t(abandon state link-id reason)  ; -> state\n   803\t```\n   804\t\n   805\tA `request` is plain data and nothing else. Its closed key set is\n   806\t`:yin.link/id`, `:yin.link/format`, `:yin.link/contract` (optional), and\n   807\texactly one of `:yin.link/name` or `:yin.link/identity`. The two\n   808\tadmissible shapes:\n   809\t\n   810\t```clojure\n   811\t;; by module name: the linker resolves the manifest (section 8)\n   812\t{:yin.link/id       [:t0 7]                  ; section 7.2, step 3\n   813\t :yin.link/format   :yin.debruijn.code\n   814\t :yin.link/contract \"b1\"\n   815\t :yin.link/name     'my.lib}\n   816\t\n   817\t;; by bare identity: B6's closed-image path, no manifest\n   818\t{:yin.link/id       [:t0 8]\n   819\t :yin.link/format   :yin.debruijn.code\n   820\t :yin.link/contract \"b1\"\n   821\t :yin.link/identity H}\n   822\t```\n   823\t\n   824\tA request carrying both `:yin.link/name` and `:yin.link/identity`, or\n   825\tneither, is `:invalid-request`, as is any key outside the set or any\n   826\tvalue that is not a scalar, keyword, symbol, address, or the id vector.\n   827\tA request carries no index, no receiver, no function, and no handle. The\n   828\trefusal is appended on the response stream under the request's id where\n   829\tthe id is well formed, and under `:yin.link/id nil` otherwise; it is\n   830\tnever processed further. The receiver's capabilities do not travel\n   831\tbecause discharge happens at the receiver (section 4.2, 5b).\n806:`:yin.link/id`, `:yin.link/format`, `:yin.link/contract` (optional), and\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-25 14:16:55 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-25 21:16:55 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\nRule R remains the right semantic direction, but the revised design is not yet a complete implementation gate. These statuses judge the proposed design; the checks have not been implemented.\n\n| Prior finding | Status | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Incoming macro store | RESOLVED | The revision checks both context construction and batch expansion, covering seeded and restored stores before `macro-of` can rewrite an application ([revision:18,51](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790343000000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-revision.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:18); [macro.cljc:973](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:973), [macro.cljc:1170](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:1170), [macro.cljc:1220](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:1220)). |\n| Direct admission | PARTLY RESOLVED | The walker’s datom, row, and raw-transition paths are now assigned checks, but the semantic datom-code loader is absent from the table ([revision:42](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790343000000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-revision.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:42); [semantic.cljc:670](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:670)). |\n| Effect and store ingress | PARTLY RESOLVED | Dispatcher, direct instructions, and constructors are covered. Portable module-store snapshots and restored scheduler updates still need an explicit check or an unreachability proof ([revision:36,52](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790343000000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-revision.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:36); [linker spec:1342](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:1342); [engine.cljc:423](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:423)). |\n| Four contract revisions | PARTLY RESOLVED | `v3/v3/b2/r2` covers the changed meanings, but validation alone does not reject every old lowered image, and direct loaders do not receive or compare a contract stamp ([revision:59-61](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790343000000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-revision.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:59); [semantic.cljc:713](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:713)). |\n| Quoted `yin/def` data | RESOLVED | The revised rule permits a literal symbol while refusing its use as a definition or store key ([revision:32-38](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790343000000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-revision.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:32)). |\n\nActionable findings:\n\nP1 | [revision:47](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790343000000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-revision.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:47) | The table covers `code/well-formed-vector?`, but `semantic/vm-load-program` loads `:yin.code/*` datoms through the distinct `code/well-formed?` validator ([semantic.cljc:529](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:529), [code.cljc:162](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/code.cljc:162), [code.cljc:370](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/code.cljc:370)). That lane can otherwise admit `:var yin/def` or reserved store operands; its runtime `:var` still uses ordinary resolution ([semantic.cljc:263](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:263)). | Add semantic datom-code admission to the table and validate reserved operands and `:define` there, with a direct-loader regression test.\n\nP1 | [revision:36](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790343000000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-revision.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:36) | The rule forbids `yin/def` in a module-store snapshot, but the ten boundaries assign no check when a receiver instantiates snapshots; the linker spec explicitly restores them into `:module-stores` ([linker spec:1333](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:1333), [linker spec:1342](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:1342)). The scheduler can also merge `:store-updates` outside the listed store-write guards ([engine.cljc:404-425](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:404)). | Assign reserved-key validation to every portable-state lower/restore boundary and to admitted scheduler updates, or prove their keys are minted non-symbol IDs. Check carried environments as well as stores before execution. This is a design obligation for the deferred linker/UCF implementation, not a claim that today’s unimplemented module-snapshot path is exploitable.\n\nP1 | [revision:61](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790343000000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-revision.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:61) | “An old image contains `:var yin/def` or `:load-free yin/def`” is not generally true: an old image with no definition can pass the new grammar. The stack and register validators judge image structure, not an incoming version ([debruijn_code.cljc:846](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc:846), [debruijn_register_code.cljc:667](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc:667)); direct loaders take no contract argument ([semantic.cljc:728](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:728), [stack.cljc:95](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc:95)). AST definition bytes can indeed be identical across revisions, so an old *stamped* AST manifest is refused by its stamp, not its body. The M2 fetch API currently accepts an omitted contract ([linker.cljc:1184-1193](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc:1184)). | Require and compare a contract at each persistent-code admission boundary for all four formats; update the optional wire/API contract and callers. Treat unversioned fresh source as an explicit separate path, not as proof that an old image was refused.\n\nP1 | [revision:91](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790343000000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-revision.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:91) | Criteria 7 and 10 require UCF lowering and a lift/lower round trip in the pre-M2 Rule R gate, but UCF is explicitly proposed/deferred and its current module provides canonicalization and safepoint helpers, not lift/lower ([UCF spec:1](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1), [ucf.cljc:387](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf.cljc:387)). No VM loader compares an incoming UCF stamp today; the ledger’s derivation-profile comparison is a different check ([ledger.cljc:197](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/yin/vm/ledger.cljc:197)). | Gate Rule R now on the implemented loaders, contract mismatch, and a local parked-during-definition resume test. Keep UCF frame encoding and old-stamp lift/lower refusal as explicit M4 acceptance criteria, unless full UCF implementation is intentionally brought into this milestone.\n\nThe table otherwise places the walker, expander, de Bruijn resolver, and central effect-dispatch checks at sensible boundaries. `dao.await`’s post-constructor store merge uses newly minted keyword keys, not the symbol `yin/def` ([await.cljc:171-183](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/dao/await.cljc:171), [await.cljc:217-223](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-phase2/src/cljc/dao/await.cljc:217)); it merits a regression assertion but is not another demonstrated shadow route. A module-registry binding of that name likewise cannot change a syntactic definition if variable occurrences are rejected before resolution. The revision’s completeness condition is therefore **not yet proved**, principally because semantic datom admission and restored state are outside its stated boundaries.\n\nI would split the proposed two-commit sequence into three independently gated changes: first, compatible safety rails for reserved store keys and macro-context ingress; second, the atomic four-engine syntax, lowering, validation, and contract cutover; third, M2 guard removal and fixture changes. The full UCF round-trip remains a later milestone. This keeps each commit coherent without putting the known M2 alias-discharge defect on the committed branch. Add explicit tests for the semantic datom loader, snapshot rejection, an old no-`yin/def` image, and the raw walker’s reserved cases; criterion 1’s “one test per boundary” is otherwise too broad to prove those paths.\n\nVerdict: REQUEST CHANGES\nSign-off: DENIED"}}
